{"step_index":1062,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T21:24:55Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-11T05:24:55+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T05:25:04+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-11T05:24:55+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T05:25:04+08:00\nThe search for \"web browser detect mobile screenshot ios safari android chrome visibilitychange blur\" returned the following summary:\n**No, a web browser (like iOS Safari or Android Chrome) cannot directly detect when a user takes a native device screenshot[1].** \n\nUnlike native mobile apps (which have access to native SDK hooks or system flags like Android's `FLAG_SECURE`), web pages run inside a strict browser sandbox[1][2]. The operating system handles hardware button shortcuts (like Volume Down + Power) entirely outside the webpage's awareness[3].\n\nHowever, developers often look at events like `visibilitychange`, `blur`, or `focus` to manage application state when a user leaves the page. Here is how those events relate to mobile workflows and why they **cannot** reliably detect screenshots.\n\n---\n\n### Why `visibilitychange` and `blur` Do Not Detect Screenshots\n\n1. **iOS Safari:** \n   * When a user presses the hardware buttons to take a screenshot on an iPhone, **it does not trigger** `visibilitychange` or `blur`. The browser stays active in the foreground while the OS overlay flashes on top. \n   * The *only* time `visibilitychange` (state changing to `hidden`) or `blur` fires on iOS is when the user actually leaves Safari—such as swiping up to go to the home screen, switching apps, or pulling down the iOS Control Center (which triggers a blur/hide depending on iOS version).\n2. **Android Chrome:**\n   * Taking a standard screenshot via hardware keys **does not** trigger a page visibility or blur event. \n   * Newer versions of Android (Android 14+) introduced a system-level *Screenshot Detection API* for native apps[4], but **this API is not exposed to web browsers or JavaScript**.\n\n---\n\n### Code Example: What `visibilitychange` *Actually* Catches\nIf you implement standard visibility tracking, it will tell you when the user minimizes the browser or switches tabs, but **not** if they snapped a screenshot[1][5]:\n\n```javascript\ndocument.addEventListener('visibilitychange', () => {\n    if (document.hidden) {\n        console.log(\"Browser tab is now hidden (User switched apps or went to home screen).\");\n        // Example: Blur sensitive content just in case\n        document.getElementById('sensitive-content').style.filter = 'blur(20px)';\n    } else {\n        console.log(\"Browser tab is visible again.\");\n        document.getElementById('sensitive-content').style.filter = 'none';\n    }\n});\n\nwindow.addEventListener('blur', () => {\n    // Triggers when the window loses focus\n    console.log(\"Window lost focus\");\n});\n```\n\n### Summary of What Is and Isn't Possible\n\n* **Can you block screenshots on Mobile Web (Safari/Chrome)?** No[1]. \n* **Can you detect screenshots via JavaScript?** No. The browser security model prevents web pages from listening to OS-level screenshot triggers[1][6].\n* **Can you use `visibilitychange` to hide content when the user leaves the page?** Yes, but it reacts to app-switching/minimizing, **not** to the moment a screenshot is taken[2][5]. If you need true screenshot prevention or detection, you must build a **Native Mobile App** (using Swift/Kotlin or React Native/Flutter native modules) rather than relying on a mobile web browser[2][7].\n\nSources:\n[1] [aivideodetector.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFGXM7KJRybpflMiVw0l8FcDSZ7DhobJ-ndl6saxI4qOXTlb1zGqo5QMk9Ri6H4xFTsrbXcooTA19F7USxRWjhiHjm0Xxm3blVPO_wpu8FozHMc5VeXIPcltkVePQ2zhLBhDbdtDmw7xBDdKm7DF2ZidiYsjHZpwQ6cpUb8mzQ=)\n[2] [peakiq.in](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF0vs_zm9e4W83jlnmivu34aGymNnUsjcNuqgzRrUBdTuKabardbselp6ys6aqcskjnLMlKFmQ08pkwZmoHHLGfnpHPGBtksHj5eAOqzEOUzWR0SziTXSxvoKoll6ya-qkJtsEW0uWae64BSZY2PXe81861z969HrwLuA==)\n[3] [aivideodetector.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFGXM7KJRybpflMiVw0l8FcDSZ7DhobJ-ndl6saxI4qOXTlb1zGqo5QMk9Ri6H4xFTsrbXcooTA19F7USxRWjhiHjm0Xxm3blVPO_wpu8FozHMc5VeXIPcltkVePQ2zhLBhDbdtDmw7xBDdKm7DF2ZidiYsjHZpwQ6cpUb8mzQ=)\n[4] [android.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEEu0ZI8K1nmHz0loOLp1fe3j57G5h7Z7bVsa5wNvOtHs7orW0_zz210a4nzK2_xoPYXwjcWKhhA3c0Nqs_m2pryZ0Ulk1EcSzQBvJj8aZ8J4vE-xOwYQEgVTQFb0o4aocpYwuFLAkjAxB-FxEzSzYo-GQg1SNBAXGaxJq0VWCikLGk)\n[5] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGHznk93fzqNQwdIl12zq4eTH6KUzaHxKOrCBTgX-gCXQecyj7xtSeOcjGpQ7NHhhTTKwPbQQIETTIMw_jxqeWdKX6sc45rUPPj4bB6Qu0CykQgbHzJz7cbcNT9V_ov9JHlYnU-Qpt6CNg0tIUM_WBXC0C6Uz0nLPdDSSwYSy5sNKKs0BQ1-h1DkGX_Ik3PSY19Ce5WS8h5KFZT-jef7gbXvSIWE8ff6qNeUA==)\n[6] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGrHKD9onFWBzaqrkw8Y9Kedz5jxXHA8i9zCDcITtxyvQ3MuwHnPVdjVf102duMw83YAwm7n-ubgNqv9r3eWlgBktBzdsNNQH-hbmLxTxSBDB-3Nrqa5NEX4uVaNAxDMuTr02Iur6r98q7Swneu-CaGaD_HbJFpUJP7h3YDd0_zqZODmNxcYrWQbw==)\n[7] [proandroiddev.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQF-0Aiuy9Dk9gpvieYsKkiIXmLMn4rlvUNltfWq3A5yB9wySAb35nNrUiSkx89ixZJp8CO7kU0_NLvnfbAruA7zLQeHDiQRZ8UQ7rmGBZFfH0mU2WhhcaRSEm85GdeZgQUdbgq9Pmsfju8JmGVkVQ3FGtf_jM_8NmC6)\n\nNote: Source URLs are HTTP redirects; check where they resolve if you need the direct link."}
